#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205005} :activity/id #uuid "34ec29ec-9746-5bc0-92a8-214a71e52bef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050940 :topic/id #uuid "738cac6f-eca5-5a18-9894-32e0395cb5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 5845} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205188 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll continue exploring the *total derivative* of a vector-valued function, which generalizes the derivative to functions with multiple inputs and outputs.\n\nSuppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/c8/c8fbc309a129b8e19db46aebff637e47259b629ef83c282a38b3b318c3a4f269.svg)\n\nThis is sometimes called the **Jacobian matrix** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$. Each row corresponds to the derivative of one component function $f_i$, and each column corresponds to the rate of change of all the components of $\\boldsymbol f$ with respect to one input variable $x_j$. This is sometimes called the **numerator layout** for derivatives because each row corresponds to each function component, which appears in the numerator positions of the partial derivatives.\n\nNotice that if $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a scalar-valued function, then the total derivative is a row vector given by\n  ![](images/6e/6ef756e82eb71d528a8f279863fd86efc8669658456a65879fba73d08e964bd5.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, if $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is a vector-valued function of a single variable, then the total derivative is a column vector\n  ![](images/cd/cd232968d5ffff3040294f8093a42c91bccd3207f91bfc2f784cb53cad00441d.svg)\n\nNow let’s get some practice by working through a few examples of total derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "56f24ef3-119b-5459-90a4-c9d67357d755" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12299 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "36efe1c6-a153-509f-9b2a-6f949e414000" :step/math-academy-id 450022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204076 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203872 :question/id #uuid "56dcfaf0-3600-5f8a-aa49-fa6bcb04ec0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-18007"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feb08dad-d927-55f9-8fef-723795030c8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530543}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shape of a Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "f8f792f3-bf19-5553-a59a-8cee18174c20" :step/math-academy-id 450023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205193 :tutorial/content "Let’s examine examples of functions defined by a matrix of constants multiplied by a vector more carefully.\n\nConsider the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined by\n![](images/f8/f81cbd9b797d1f1b05edd5f254d6735456e937355fe0e6692a4a830da925351b.svg) \n\nSuppose we want to compute the total derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}$ at the point ![](images/cd/cd0b094634acde36ee93c2fb90c6f11ab56c2f675d27ba65077542d75cb451d6.svg).\n\nThe total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n![](images/63/6305cfe857f2228d471b85f859302a43046470f1d182b34abbf3ab4b362e7e07.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where ![](images/a8/a8d36d7195af007d90a4f62cf213e28c7f51ced97168fb16d31a2c9192799b53.svg), we get\n![](images/79/79110c30e4f827ccc401c0d8d9b8a08f35a88d12781d83c7e365171de0bbd67d.svg)\n\nFrom here, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(\\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1 + 0 \\cdot x_2, \\qquad f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 - x_2,\\qquad f_3(\\mathbf{x}) = 0 \\cdot x_1 + 2x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is independent of $\\mathbf x,$ we also have\n\n![](images/c1/c187256b1ad2181f53482829d4d5076b0b09fc19f1d399aa41fb9da6ca0bc8f3.svg)\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ is constant and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = A}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ece2ba6-41e2-5766-ad9c-96ea0a7180a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12446 :tutorial/title "Some Standard Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "01dc7d7b-927d-590a-87fd-8577ded3bfa1" :step/math-academy-id 450027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204077 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203865 :question/id #uuid "1b970d71-b996-5a49-b024-052c32073518" :question/math-academy-id "e-17864"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d786586f-cacb-5781-86ff-cc8acf311dcf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539024}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Total Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "b64282fd-cb21-557c-a790-ca822543dd78" :step/math-academy-id 450028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205189 :tutorial/content "We've learned that the derivative of a vector-valued function is a Jacobian matrix. Now imagine the input to our function $\\mathbf f$ is a matrix instead of a vector. In such cases, the Jacobian matrix must expand to account for two input dimensions.\n\nSuppose we have a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^p$ that maps a matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, defined as\n\n![](images/f7/f7b9ca5880f164c28b490fd4a215a56769e426bdde8111a24e6530c03d83b937.svg)\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a scalar-valued function of all the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nSince the input $\\mathbf{A}$ varies along two dimensions (rows and columns), and the output $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A})$ varies along one (a vector with $p$ components), the total derivative of $\\mathbf f,$ which we denote as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}}\n$$\n\nis naturally represented as a *three-dimensional object!* The technical name for this object is a **tensor**. Since it is a three-dimensional object, this particular tensor is a **third-order tensor** (or $3$-**tensor**).\n\nTo capture a meaningful derivative, we must differentiate each component $f_i$ with respect to *every element of the matrix* $\\mathbf A.$ We can visualize this as a stack of matrices, or layers, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d533d4d2e387c8c9f177b9dafe939b58c1f1d7db2f03e9df2059fa9f7cbe22c5.png)\n\nLet's apply this concept to a specific example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "b89cedd8-d9d0-543f-82d2-8961bb1f4278" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12382 :tutorial/title "Tensor Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "bb009f64-4cfd-537e-95ac-4085ce5b648e" :step/math-academy-id 450032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204078 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203867 :question/id #uuid "981a1177-016a-55ca-9ea7-a92220087d8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-17947"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15fada7f-4a9a-5e72-9699-694e302beef3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538934}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a 3-Tensor Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "954df139-40b5-52fc-8c4e-0dcc5c046a4b" :step/math-academy-id 450033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205216 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^{3\\times2} \\to \\mathbb R^{2},$ defined as\n\n![](images/8d/8d7f629f4b82087a1d23ef1253ce7eb98ca760310d92a2ac8cf8c3fd3a4d9473.svg)\n\nWe calculate the derivatives of $\\mathbf f$ layer by layer as follows:\n\n- **Layer 1**: The derivatives of the first component $f_1$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/c4/c4dab9446396e82d20ebbb38e3aabce9908565aeb7aeb48f2804a271d6ddf66f.svg)\n\n- **Layer 2**: The derivatives of the second component $f_2$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/ce/ce7e31c8c3faf75aa7980a0e4f5f707f84f116e97b30c8b0dbd504bcfb4822c8.svg)\n\nCombining these layers into a 3D block, we get the total derivative (a $3$-tensor in $\\mathbb R^{2\\times3\\times2}$):\n\n![](images/3d/3d6eeebb92218d4f0995dbccd74b444da112f0e55b22ade0bbaa0259e06e9bb0.png)\n\nYou've probably realized by now that working with 3-tensors directly is cumbersome! So, rather than trying to visualize the full tensor all at once, focusing on its components is often helpful. Generally,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}} \\right)_{i, j, k} = \\dfrac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a_{jk}}\n$$\n\nis a *scalar* representing the derivative of the $i$th output component with respect to the element $a_{jk}$ of the matrix $\\mathbf A.$\n\nFor example, the derivative of $f_{\\color{red}1}$ with respect to $a_{{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2}}$ corresponds to the component at index $({\\color{red}1},{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2})$ of a tensor. In our example, we saw that\n![](images/4f/4fa3d21f5d62bd01491c49b3351cdd3cf751a7ad793509d1c2b3846e4f6825cd.svg)\n\nLooking at the ${\\color{red}1}$st layer (front face), row ${\\color{blue}3},$ column ${\\color{blue}2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf A}\\right)_{{\\color{red}1}, {\\color{blue}3}, {\\color{blue}2}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}.\n$$\n\nDerivatives of matrix functions arise in optimization problems, machine learning (e.g., backpropagation through layers), and physics (e.g., tensor fields). Understanding their structure is key to efficient computation and interpretation." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f4a75d3-35bf-59fd-adcb-45b912cba094" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14941 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "287be2d5-e4f5-5ea0-bca4-5e37ba7ca236" :step/math-academy-id 610778 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205010}}] :activity/title "Total and Tensor Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204180}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539068} :activity/id #uuid "c90a6945-859b-55ba-8539-f8f5fc69e031" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050940 :topic/id #uuid "738cac6f-eca5-5a18-9894-32e0395cb5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 5845} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014d9c3e-1663-5259-9277-a7bbddf6e2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538237} {:answer/id #uuid "34bbe26e-bcba-5583-9c68-b3f83a035eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538339} {:answer/id #uuid "def4b6c6-b6f4-5d5a-8261-aa0965a945ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538743} {:answer/id #uuid "e11583b8-4e30-5e78-b665-3691a908779c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538747} {:answer/id #uuid "e681a790-14d2-5d84-85a8-796a2c566dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 3x \\\\ -5x \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c80c0f77-3e67-5a7e-8ad6-7ef450e73970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee17a4e4-1d8d-5373-a5f7-48bfde731f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-269516" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{4},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{4}$ defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}x+2 \\\\ 3x \\\\ 4x-10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{4\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "54f4cfdc-4e79-588a-b324-d92edafe0a57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6cf4237-3068-5ef2-966f-556a26a2b8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538793} :answer-field/id #uuid "496c72b0-90f2-51f5-a462-e8c20d943af2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "20f2946c-2b05-548e-8d4f-c195e7f5a6e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-269858" :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{11}+3{a}_{12}-{a}_{13} \\\\ 2{a}_{21}+3{a}_{22}-{a}_{23} \\\\ 2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{31}}(2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33})=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62c410ab-c0af-598a-a7a3-56564b714e17"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89ee6b6c-075c-595a-aac5-273cdbc81bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203390} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0665a6-5fd8-5a60-a295-8eb83a2e097c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203394} {:answer/id #uuid "28fad7c5-7710-53c9-96dc-3a22b189fd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{x}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538318} {:answer/id #uuid "36ecfad0-45a6-5a26-931b-31fd7b5c023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{T}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538345} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6fd27f-675e-55fe-8e51-a1443e1cde27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538805} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5e576a-9d0c-5e63-ad25-093924cf3622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9b60135-10d8-57c5-ae3d-61cf69876b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-268177" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{T}x.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3})\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{T}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77f86be7-dd12-584e-b354-ecf9e0375cee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f51f1a-4d67-51a2-a688-b61e6a787b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538285} {:answer/id #uuid "2e584046-9915-5986-8dd3-f1cf10ec80b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538328} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea1e5d3-2f94-5af0-9b2b-dbe977709a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538397} {:answer/id #uuid "52208163-567f-5131-b309-cfa086f0b55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538408} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe8c4b7-c3a3-5549-b3d0-a5b9714b08d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538408} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c166e6-1f28-5e02-9955-3d8f3525359c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67046761-6838-5e5c-bd1f-8e1ae02d3dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-269555" :question/problem "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ gives the same matrix."} :step/id #uuid "af19e67d-7aed-57c3-8603-6dcf4cb7a6aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de143918-a5c5-54c6-8191-100ca3eb3d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a scalar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203409} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc4f1f8-61e0-5902-87da-4c880d27639d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a column vector of shape $2\\times 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538524} {:answer/id #uuid "886bd58a-d2a1-58c4-85bb-21bcd3179800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a matrix of shape $1\\times 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538548} {:answer/id #uuid "89f6a51d-2ad5-50de-abf7-2ae76d9a88e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a matrix of shape $2\\times 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538553} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5950a8-970d-5df3-b935-f039ec4c769a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a row vector of shape $1\\times 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538772}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538524} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e2f6df-668f-58d0-af45-8b0dc73f1847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab16b06c-77e8-52fd-a1bb-c0353a01d556" :question/math-academy-id "q-268488" :question/problem "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a column vector of shape $2\\times 1.$"} :step/id #uuid "ceaf80b9-2e1d-5b78-946c-a77a528f9445" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539061}}] :activity/title "Total and Tensor Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538228}]}
